{"id":"8f68d322-df08-4354-8db4-d60e25f55d5a","object":"chat.completion","created":1784984132,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid payload')\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n.get('id')\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError('Invalid node id')\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None and not isinstance(parent, str):\\n            raise ValueError('Invalid parent')\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    # find root (parent is None)\\n    root_ids = [nid for nid, n in node_map.items() if n.get('parent') is None]\\n    if len(root_ids) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError('No single root')\\n    root_id = root_ids[0]\\n    # validate required symbols exist\\n    all_symbols = {n['symbol'] for n in node_map.values()}\\n    for s in required_symbols:\\n        if s not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {s} not present')\\n    # initial retained set\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    # deep copy for processing order\\n    node_list = list(node_map.values())\\n    # sort by depth descending, then by id ascending\\n    node_list.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    # function to collect retained subtree nodes (including node itself)\\n    def collect_subtree(nid):\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            return set()\\n        result = {nid}\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            for child in children_map.get(cur, []):\\n                if child in retained:\\n                    result.add(child)\\n                    stack.append(child)\\n        return result\\n    for n in node_list:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # compute subtree that would be removed\\n        subtree_set = collect_subtree(nid)\\n        # symbols in subtree\\n        subtree_symbols = {node_map[sid]['symbol'] for sid in subtree_set}\\n        # for each required symbol, check if still present outside subtree\\n        can_remove = True\\n        for rs in required_symbols:\\n            if rs in subtree_symbols:\\n                # look for rs in retained nodes outside subtree\\n                found_outside = any(\\n                    node_map[sid]['symbol'] == rs and sid not in subtree_set\\n                    for sid in retained\\n                )\\n                if not found_outside:\\n                    can_remove = False\\n                    break\\n        if can_remove:\\n            # remove the whole subtree from retained\\n            retained -= subtree_set\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n    # sort retained ids\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce Python source code for `solve(case)` that implements grammar-guided node reduction based on the given task description. The problem: We have a tree of nodes with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The goal is to remove removable nodes (subject to constraints) to reduce the tree while preserving required_symbols (list of symbols that must appear in the remaining tree). The root is not removable. Also nodes that are not removable cannot be removed. The algorithm: process nodes in order of decreasing depth (deeper first), and within same depth, by node id (stable tie-break). For each removable node, tentatively remove it if: (a) it's not root, (b) all required symbols would still be present in the tree after removal, (c) its children? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Wait: \"a node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node that still has children that are retained? That is a bit ambiguous. In typical tree reduction, you can remove a node if its children are also removed (or if you replace it with something). But here it says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if we remove a node, we must also remove all its descendants? No, if we remove a node, we would also remove its subtree. But then it says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So removing a node might remove symbols from its subtree. That's fine. But the condition \"node with retained children\" means if any child is still in the tree (i.e., not removed yet) then we cannot remove the parent? Actually, we process deeper nodes first, so children are considered before parent. So when considering a parent, its children may already have been removed (if they were removable and conditions allowed). If any child is retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove the parent because that would remove retained children. So we need to track which nodes are retained (initially all nodes, then we mark removed). For each removable node in descending depth order, check:\n- It's not root (root id is \"root\"? but we can check parent is null? or symbol \"root\"? In fixtures, root has id \"root\", parent null, depth 0, removable false. So we can skip if not removable or parent is None? But also check if it's required symbol? The required_symbols list includes \"root\" and \"s1\". \"root\" is always present, so cannot remove root anyway. But a node with symbol in required_symbols cannot be removed (even if removable field says true? Actually the field removable may be true but the constraint says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So we must enforce that even if removable field is true, we cannot remove if its symbol is in required_symbols. So we check: if node.symbol in required_symbols, skip removal.\n- Also, ensure that after removal, all required symbols still exist in the tree (including the current node's subtree if it had a required symbol? If the node itself is not a required symbol but its subtree contains a required symbol, removing it would remove that required symbol, so we must check that the required symbols are still present in the remaining tree. We can compute the set of symbols present in all retained nodes (including ancestors and other branches). We need to compute which symbols are present in the current set of retained nodes. Initially all nodes are retained. When we consider removing a node, we need to see if the required symbols are still present after removing this node and all its retained descendants (since removing a node implies its entire subtree is gone). But careful: if some descendants have already been removed, they are already gone. So the removed subtree is the node itself and any descendants that are still retained (since they would be removed along with it). So we need to calculate the set of symbols that would be lost: the node's symbol plus the symbols of all its retained descendants. If any of those symbols are required and not present elsewhere in the tree, then removal is not allowed. So we need to compute for each node the subtree symbols present in the current retained set.\n\nWe need to do this efficiently: build a mapping from node id to node object, and also build children lists. Then process nodes in decreasing depth, and for each node, if conditions allow, remove it (mark as removed, update retained set). At the end, we have list of removed node ids (in order of removal) and retained node ids.\n\nOutput schema: {\"removed_node_ids\": array of strings (removal order), \"retained_node_ids\": array of strings (sorted? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (maybe alphabetically? or by depth? The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs\" so likely sorted by id (string sort). The removal order is the order in which nodes were removed (the order we process them).\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build dict node_by_id.\n- Build dict children_by_id (list of child IDs).\n- Compute initial set of retained node IDs (all nodes).\n- For each node, we need to quickly compute the set of symbols in its retained subtree. We can precompute total subtree symbols for each node initially, but as we remove descendants, the subtree symbols change. Since we process depth decreasing, when we consider a node, its descendants have already been considered (deeper first), so some may have been removed. We need to know which descendants are still retained. We can maintain a set of retained node IDs. For each node, we can compute the set of symbols in its retained subtree by traversing its retained descendants (but that could be O(n^2) worst case). However, n is small (only 7 nodes in examples). We can just collect all retained descendants for each node when needed.\n\nSimpler: For each node in the ordered list (descending depth, tie by id), we check if its symbol is required -> skip. If not removable -> skip. If it's root (parent None) -> skip. Then compute the set of retained node IDs in its subtree (including itself). If any of those are required symbols (i.e., the symbol of the node or its retained descendants is in required_symbols) and that symbol is not present elsewhere in the retained tree outside this subtree, then removal would lose the required symbol. So we need to check: For each required symbol, is there at least one retained node with that symbol not in this subtree? If the subtree contains all occurrences of