rent ID to removed_node_ids. But we still need to mark them as not retained. So the order is the order we processed nodes and decided to remove them.\n\nAlso, we must ensure that when we remove a node, its children are also removed from retained set, so that when we later check other nodes (like its siblings or ancestors), the tree state reflects the removal. So we need to update retained set accordingly.\n\nAfter processing all nodes, we have retained_node_ids (all node IDs not removed), sorted. Return.\n\nEdge case: If any required symbol is missing from the initial tree, that is infeasible? The input should have them present. But we should validate: after processing, if any required symbol not present, raise ValueError? But the problem says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" and \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". So we should check that initially all required symbols exist in the tree. Also check that nodes have required fields (id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility). Also check that root is not removable. etc.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that receives the payload (dict with \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\"). Return dict with keys \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nNow produce the source code as a JSON string within an object with key \"implementation\". Must be valid Python.\n\nI'll write code in a simple way, assuming small trees (n<100). Use sets and dicts.\n\nSteps:\n1. Validate payload: must have \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list.\n2. Build node map, children map, and set of all node ids.\n3. Track retained set = set of all node ids.\n4. Identify root node (parent is None or id \"root\"? Actually root could have different id, but in fixtures it's \"root\". But we should not assume. Better find node with parent None. If more than one or none, error.\n5. Validate required_symbols: each must exist in node symbols at least once.\n6. Create list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending (string sort). Use the actual node objects.\n7. For each node in that order:\n   - if node.id not in retained: continue (already removed as part of ancestor removal)\n   - if node.id == root id: continue (never remove root)\n   - if not node.removable: continue\n   - if node.symbol in required_symbols: continue\n   - Compute the set of retained descendant IDs (including self) via BFS/DFS limited to retained nodes.\n   - For each required symbol, check if any retained node outside this subtree has that symbol. If all occurrences of a required symbol are inside this subtree, then removal would lose that symbol -> not allowed.\n   - If allowed, then:\n        - Remove all nodes in that subtree from retained set.\n        - Append node.id to removed_node_ids list.\n8. After loop, sorted_retained = sorted(retained set) (as strings)\n9. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained}\n\nNote: The descendants that are removed as part of parent removal: they are removed from retained set but not added to removed_node_ids individually. That is consistent with \"actual removal order\" being only the nodes we explicitly decide to remove. However, if a descendant was already processed earlier and was not removed because it was not allowed, but later its parent is removed, that descendant gets removed. That is fine. It should not be in removed_node_ids because we never decided to remove it; it was removed as a side effect. The output just wants the IDs of nodes we removed (the decisions). So that's fine.\n\nTest with the given fixtures: In first case, nodes: n2 removable true, n4 removable true, n6 removable true. Required symbols: root, s1. n2 has symbol s2 (not required), its subtree includes n4 (symbol s1, required). Is s1 represented elsewhere? n1 also has s1. So removing n2 would lose n4's s1 but n1 still has s1. So allowed. n4 removable true, symbol s1 (required) -> can't remove because symbol required. n6 removable true, symbol s0 not required, its subtree only itself. s0 is not required. But does removing n6 lose any required symbol? No. However, n6's parent n1 has symbol s1 required, which is outside. So allowed. But need to check processing order: depth: root0, n1 depth1, n2 depth1, n3 depth1, n4 depth2, n5 depth2, n6 depth2. Descending: process depth2 first: n4, n5, n6. n4 symbol s1 required -> skip. n5 removable false -> skip. n6 removable true, symbol s0 not required, subtree only itself, check required symbols: s1 and root are present elsewhere (n1, root). So remove n6. Then depth1: n1, n2, n3. n1 removable false skip. n2 removable true, its retained subtree includes n4? But n4 is still retained because we didn't remove it. So subtree of n2 includes nodes n2 itself and n4 (and maybe others? n2 children: n4 only). So symbols: s2 (n2) and s1 (n4). s1 is required. Is there another s1? n1 still has s1. So allowed. Remove n2 and its subtree (n2 and n4). n4 is removed as consequence. Then removed_node_ids = [n6, n2]. Then n3 not removable. root not removable. Retained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5 (since n5 is child of n3, not removed). Sorted: n1, n3, n5, root. That matches? The fixture doesn't show expected output but we can infer. That seems plausible.\n\nNow implement code.\n\nWe must be careful with imports: only standard library. No random, os, etc.\n\nNow produce the final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":3851,"total_tokens":6020,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3081},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}